Checkpoints for the long run are already in place. After every epoch, train.py overwrites last.pt, writes best.pt when the validation loss improves, and logs eta plus remaining_epochs. Stopping the process and restarting with --resume on that last.pt continues from the next epoch. Do not resume from runs/pilot/last.pt; that checkpoint was trained on neuron ids.

The next step is one training run only: config full, seed 0, on the cleft z-splits.

python src/train.py --data-dir ./data/cremi_train --val-data-dir ./data/cremi_val --dataset cremi --config-name full --seed 0 --out-dir ./runs/cremi_full_seed0 --max-epochs 600 --iterations-per-epoch 100 --early-stop-patience 50
That uses all three volumes, z 0:70 for training and z 70:100 for validation. Test z 100:125 stays unused until inference. From the pilot, 100 steps is about 7–8 minutes per epoch, so 600 epochs is up to about 3 days unless validation stops it earlier. Watch the first few eta= lines before starting any other config or seed. Switch to Agent mode if you want this run started.





do we really need to do 600 epochs?

or is there any no. where those no. of epochs are enough for proper efficiency?

No. 600 is the maximum budget this script uses so the learning-rate schedule matches reference [26]. It is not a measured “enough” point. Nothing in this project has shown that training needs all 600, or that some smaller fixed number is the efficient one.

The learning rate is 0.01 * (1 - epoch / max_epochs)^0.9. If you set --max-epochs 100, the rate falls six times faster than it does with 600. That is a different training schedule, not the same run stopped early.

The mechanism that shortens the run without changing that schedule is early stopping. With --max-epochs 600 and --early-stop-patience 50, training keeps the 600-epoch learning-rate curve, writes best.pt whenever validation loss improves, and stops 50 epochs after it stops improving. The epoch you would report is whatever epoch best.pt came from. That number is not knowable until this cleft split has a validation curve. The old 10-epoch pilot was neuron ids, so it does not answer it.

